Round 2865.decision_summary:
R2865 starts the build of open-debt item 751, the pin's region model, as the first step of the order the owner fixed on 2026-09-21: build the region model, move routing onto it, and only then remove the HRW election. The atom was chosen by measuring rather than picking. The census queue is empty (rc=0). transport-shm's base is upstream (item 823, SCE), so this round keeps its hands off it. routing-router, routing-peer and routing-routes, three of the 13 remaining atoms, all stand on 751, and it is flagged critical.

The pin was read directly (checkout resolved by upstream_citation_anchor_gate.upstream_root()). Three facts decided the design:
- `gateway.rs` @ `GatewaySouthConf::Preset(GatewayPresetConf::Auto) => match mode {` builds North plus the default south subregions per mode, then Local.
- `@ match (region.bound(), region.mode().unwrap_or(mode)) {` chooses the hat per region.
- The router hat's `new_transport_unicast_face` adds a link for EVERY face it owns, whatever its whatami. Its `handle_oam` asserts that a non-router in North has a south remote bound.

The round therefore makes the region the router's face classifier rather than storing an unread field. `face_region` (compute_region_of for a router node) feeds `tier_of(region)`, which reads the ported hat table. For every remote that announces no bound, which is every stock node and every wz node, this is the old whatami table exactly. The one case that now classifies differently is a remote announcing that we are south of it, and it lands in North as the pin places it.

The round plan the next rounds follow was derived by mapping the pin onto wz:
(2) nets keyed by RegionMap instead of FaceTier;
(3) per-region entity stores with the register-in-owner + propagate(other_info) dispatcher loop;
(4) the linkstate GWY bit;
(5) the data plane's InterRegionFilter (primary gateway = max zid) beside is_master;
(6) the query plane;
(7) tokens and interests;
(8) the router's peer region to the pin's semantics (full_linkstate off, pull-only south peers), a wire change;
(9) removing election.

Round 2879.decision_summary:
R2879 takes open-debt item 751 step 5, the one R2878's carry named: the data plane's inter-region filter, the first consumer of the gateway bit R2878 put on the wire.

The pin decides every data egress that crosses a region boundary with one rule (`zenoh/src/net/routing/dispatcher/tables.rs` @ `pub(crate) struct InterRegionFilter<'a> {`). Same side of the boundary: pass. Otherwise the candidates are the gateways of the destination neighbour (source north) or of the forwarder (source south); no candidates: pass; a candidate that is the message's own origin: drop; else only the largest zid carries it (`@ let primary = gwys.iter().max().unwrap();`). A broker hat has no gateway view, so a client region always passes (`zenoh/src/net/routing/hat/broker/mod.rs` @ `fn gateways(&self, _tables: &TablesData) -> Option<Vec<ZenohIdProto>> {`). `routing_region.rs` now carries it arm for arm as `InterRegionFilter::resolve`, generic over a `GatewayView` the router implements from its hats' nets.

The step plan said "beside `is_master`". That turned out not to be a place the filter could sit: both gates decide the same crossing, the election per keyexpr by hash and the filter per forwarder by zid order, so requiring both would let each pick a different router and let a Push cross nowhere. The judgement is therefore: at every data crossing the filter REPLACES the election, which stays only on the query plane until step 6 and is removed at step 9. Four sites move together, because the double-delivery argument ties them:
- the peer-to-router bridge takes the filter with the forwarder's gateways (one decision per Push);
- the router-to-peer bridge takes it per egress neighbour, as the pin passes `dst_zid: Some(&dir.dst_face.zid)`;
- the client-delivery defer is keyed on the same peer-to-router decision: a router that does not carry a peer-source Push north does not deliver it, because the carrier's copy comes back to it from the router mesh as a router source;
- the client re-injection's router leg is no longer gated: a client sits below one router, that router carries its Put into both meshes, and the copy another gateway then receives through the peer mesh is dropped there, its origin being a gateway.
`push_origin` resolves the origin and the forwarder in the inbound net, the pin's `remote_node_id_to_zid`.

Two things moved with the semantics rather than around them. The E2E `wz_router_hat_non_master_defers_a_client_double_delivery` was built on HRW with fixed zids and a keyexpr chosen to make R1 the non-master; it now fixes R2 as the larger gateway and has the publisher dial both routers, so R1 defers for every keyexpr. Its name is kept because the `router-hat-router` atom's reason cites it. The `router-master-election` cross-impl marker on zenohd interop leg 10 claimed that forcing `is_master` false reds that pubsub bridge, which is no longer true; it moved to the pico query leg, whose client query enters the router mesh through the master-gated query block. That move is read from `mesh_query_block`, not measured.
